{"qid":"Q173282","names":["Khazaria"],"title":"Khazaria","endonym":null,"capsule":"Khazaria was a Turkic semi-nomadic empire occupying the steppe corridor between the Caspian and Black Seas, attested from roughly 630 to 969 CE, with its capital at Itil near the Volga delta. At its apex in the 8th–9th centuries it controlled trade arteries linking Byzantium, the Islamic world, and northern Europe, levying transit tolls on furs, slaves, and silk. The Khazar ruling elite famously converted to Judaism in the mid-8th century — attested in correspondence with Hasdai ibn Shaprut c. 960 — though the breadth of that conversion remains debated. In 965 the Kievan prince Svyatoslav I destroyed Itil and shattered Khazar power, ending the khaganate's role as a Eurasian commercial broker.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The western steppe heartland between the Don and Volga is reasonably attested through Byzantine diplomatic sources, Arabic geographers (Ibn Fadlan, al-Masudi), and numismatic finds; the northern and eastern extent into the Ural steppe is conjectural and based on tributary relationships described in literary sources rather than firm administrative boundaries.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Khazar","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Khazar"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Khazars","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khazars"}],"britannica":"topic/Khazar","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Khazars","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Irbis","nativeName":"Irbis","roman":"Irbis","start":618,"end":628,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Tong Yabghu Qaghan","nativeName":"統葉護